\f3\fs26 By the time Cracker and Sarah made it back to street level a mob had gathered and swollen to a dense unending thicket of humanity, sucking the very life out of the air. Gasping for breath Cracker and Sarah waded into the vast seething mass and, although their progress frustratingly slow, they incrementally plotted a course through many thousands of street people, vendors, whores and shit-bags towards the throbbing lights of the entertainment district. Rank fumes assaulted Sarah\'92s nose and eyes, making both run uncontrollably, obliging her to tear a square of fabric from her skirt for a makeshift handkerchief. Putrid airborne particles violated her pristine, unblemished skin, producing a maddening desire to scratch, which she did not do, not once. Analyzing the ebb and flow of the crowd, as a distraction from her screaming skin, she was soon able to predict the path of least resistance having realised, in this environment, the quickest way between two points was not a straight line. Shuffling sideways or even backwards, at the right moment, when the cadence of the crowd changed, provided an opportunity to take advantage of a surge or avoid a crush. As advancement accelerated, they became increasingly aware that it was becoming rather damp underfoot and the brown water seemed to be rising at an alarming rate. \
\'93Is it usually like this?\'94 Sarah asked, genuinely surprised by the enormity of the crowd.\
\'93No. The security zone around the explosion must have been expanded and they are all being funneled this way. We have got to get out of here; it\'92s going to get dangerous.\'94 Cracker was on clean up at \'93The Big Crush\'94 six years previous. The crowd had been so vast that thousands were crushed or suffocated where they stood and he could never forget the macabre sight of the dead, all standing, melded tightly together by rigor mortis in huge human sheaves. Wedging them apart took so much time that they were ordered to simply scoop them up with a front-end loader and dump them in a truck. A very gruesome duty, considering the odd few were still alive. He had met Shakti after ducking off to throw up only to find the new police recruit already occupying the best spot, upchucking what appeared to be an exceedingly un-chewed steak. \
\'93Is that real beef?\'94 He had said, suddenly hungry.\
\'93You can have it. I don\'92t think I\'92ll need it.\'94 Shakti said gagging, his abdomen jerking with uncontrollable spasms. \
\'93Na. I\'92ll only spew it up as soon as I get back to work. I don\'92t think people are meant to see things like that.\'94\
\'93Things like that should not happen.\'94\
They hadn\'92t actually become friends that night, Shakti lacked the ability to connect, but they had become allies. The shared distress over the fatalities of \'93The Big Crush\'94 was the foundation of their alliance, also the starting point of their respective journeys to claustrophobia. \

